View `s.mulIndicator f` as the piecewise function
\[
x \mapsto \begin{cases} f(x),&x\in s,\\ 1,&x\notin s.\end{cases}
\]

Prove the second, more general statement first via a frontier-gluing principle. On `interior s` the function is locally `f`; outside `closure s` it is locally the constant `1`; and at a frontier point `a`, the hypothesis `f a = 1` makes the two branches agree. Continuity there follows by taking a neighborhood of `1`: continuity of `f` gives a smaller neighborhood on which `f` lies in it, while the other branch is already exactly `1`.

The useful intermediate goals are:

- identify `mulIndicator` with this piecewise representation;
- establish continuity on the interior, exterior, and frontier;
- combine these cases into global continuity.

Then obtain the clopen case from the general statement: a clopen set has empty frontier, so the frontier agreement condition is vacuous. The essential assumption in the general case is precisely `f = 1` on the frontier; without branch agreement, the piecewise function can jump there.

